# Frontend Environment Configuration
# Copy this file to .env for local development

# Network Configuration
# For local development (Hardhat): 1337
# For Polygon Amoy testnet: 80002
# For Polygon mainnet: 137
# For Mordor testnet (Ethereum Classic): 63
# For Ethereum Classic mainnet: 61
VITE_NETWORK_ID=137

# RPC URL for connecting to blockchain
# Local: http://localhost:8545
# Polygon Amoy: https://rpc-amoy.polygon.technology
# Polygon mainnet: https://polygon-bor-rpc.publicnode.com
# Mordor: https://rpc.mordor.etccooperative.org
# Ethereum Classic: https://etc.rivet.link
#
# NOTE: VITE_RPC_URL is a GLOBAL override applied to EVERY chain. Leave it unset to
# use the correct per-chain default for VITE_NETWORK_ID (recommended). Only set it to
# pin one RPC, and keep it consistent with the chain selected above (e.g. a Polygon
# URL when VITE_NETWORK_ID=137 — otherwise a 137 app would read from an Amoy RPC).
# VITE_RPC_URL=

# --- Indexing subgraph (Spec 017: v2 WagerRegistry + WagerTransfer) ---
# Subgraph endpoints are now resolved PER-CHAIN in src/config/networks.js, so a
# network without an indexer (e.g. Ethereum Classic Mordor) transparently reads
# wagers directly from the WagerRegistry over RPC (RegistrySource) — no global
# flag required. Defaults ship for the indexed networks; override per-chain only
# if you run your own indexer:
#   VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_POLYGON  (137)   default: studio fairwins-polygon
#   VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_AMOY     (80002) default: studio fairwins-amoy
#   VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_MORDOR   (63)    default: none → RPC reads (no subgraph)
# (Studio dev query endpoints are rate-limited; for production publish to the
#  decentralized network and use the gateway URL.)
# VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_POLYGON=
# VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_AMOY=
# VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL_MORDOR=
#
# Legacy single-endpoint override. Applies ONLY to the build-time active chain
# (VITE_NETWORK_ID) and never leaks to other networks at runtime. Prefer the
# per-chain vars above.
# VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL=
# Force a specific wager data source for debugging: subgraph | registry | events.
# Leave unset to auto-select per chain (subgraph where indexed, RPC otherwise).
# VITE_WAGER_SOURCE=
#
# The Graph Studio DEPLOY KEY is a secret used only by `graph auth` when deploying
# the subgraph — it is NOT a frontend var and MUST live in your local .env only,
# never committed.

# --- Mordor (Ethereum Classic testnet, chainId 63) overrides (Spec 015) ---
# All optional; networks.js ships sensible defaults. Set these to point at a
# custom RPC, the verified Classic USD (USC) token, or ETCswap so the in-app
# swap turns on. The DEX/swap capability stays OFF (and the swap UI hidden)
# until ALL of FACTORY + SWAP_ROUTER + QUOTER + WETC are provided — no mock DEX.
# VITE_RPC_URL_MORDOR=https://rpc.mordor.etccooperative.org
# VITE_MORDOR_USC=0xDE093684c796204224BC081f937aa059D903c52a   # VERIFY on-chain (T001)
# VITE_MORDOR_ETCSWAP_FACTORY=
# VITE_MORDOR_ETCSWAP_SWAP_ROUTER=
# VITE_MORDOR_ETCSWAP_QUOTER=
# VITE_MORDOR_ETCSWAP_POSITION_MANAGER=
# VITE_MORDOR_WETC=
# VITE_MORDOR_FAUCET=                                          # test-ETC faucet (T003)
# VITE_MORDOR_ETCSWAP_URL=https://etcswap.org

# --- Ethereum Classic mainnet (chainId 61) overrides (Spec 033) ---
# All optional; networks.js ships on-chain-verified ETCswap V3 defaults so swaps
# work out of the box. Set these only to point at a custom RPC/token or a
# different ETCswap deployment. Like every chain, blanking a required address
# (FACTORY + SWAP_ROUTER + QUOTER + WETC) turns the DEX/swap capability OFF — no
# mock DEX. ETC mainnet enables ONLY the swap surface (it is wager-legacy).
# VITE_RPC_URL_ETC=https://etc.rivet.link
# Last resort behind VITE_RPC_URL_ETC for members who have configured no endpoint of their
# own — ETC is served by community-run RPCs, so the primary going dark would otherwise leave
# the chain unreadable. Must send CORS headers for browser use.
# VITE_RPC_URL_ETC_FAILOVER=https://etc.etcdesktop.com
# VITE_ETC_USC=0xDE093684c796204224BC081f937aa059D903c52a       # Classic USD (USC), 6 decimals
# VITE_ETC_ETCSWAP_FACTORY=0x2624E907BcC04f93C8f29d7C7149a8700Ceb8cDC
# VITE_ETC_ETCSWAP_SWAP_ROUTER=0xEd88EDD995b00956097bF90d39C9341BBde324d1
# VITE_ETC_ETCSWAP_QUOTER=0x4d8c163400CB87Cbe1bae76dBf36A09FED85d39B
# VITE_ETC_ETCSWAP_POSITION_MANAGER=0x3CEDe6562D6626A04d7502CC35720901999AB699
# VITE_ETC_WETC=0x1953cab0E5bFa6D4a9BaD6E05fD46C1CC6527a5a       # Wrapped ETC, 18 decimals
# VITE_ETC_ETCSWAP_URL=https://v3.etcswap.org

# --- QuickNode-primary / publicnode-failover RPC overrides, every EVM mainnet (release 1.14.0
#     task 8) ---
# `rpcUrl` for the five EVM mainnets below (Polygon 137, Ethereum 1, Optimism 10, Base 8453,
# Arbitrum One 42161) reads a per-chain VITE_RPC_URL_<CHAIN> override; the COMMITTED DEFAULT stays
# the current public (publicnode.com) URL — never a key. Set VITE_RPC_URL_<CHAIN> at deploy time to
# a QuickNode (or other keyed) endpoint to get archive depth / higher rate limits.
#
# Every one of the five ALSO now declares a distinct `rpcFailoverUrl` (default: a second public
# provider, drpc.org) so a keyed primary going dark degrades to that route instead of leaving the
# chain unreadable — mirrors the ETC failover below and the relay-gateway's own Polygon pair
# (services/relay-gateway/src/config/chains.js: publicnode primary, drpc.org secondary). Both
# resolve through the ONE seam, `frontend/src/lib/network/rpcEndpoints.js#resolveRpcEndpoints`
# (spec 069), consumed by both `utils/rpcProvider.js` (ethers reads) and `wagmi.js` (wallet
# transports) — never hand-build a provider from `NETWORKS[chainId].rpcUrl` directly. A MEMBER'S
# own configured endpoint (Network settings) always takes precedence over any of this.
#
# ⚠️ `VITE_` values compile into the client bundle and are public once shipped (spec 097) — a
# QuickNode URL set here is NOT a secret the way `RPC_URL_PRIMARY_137` is on the gateway/finops/
# alto (services/relay-gateway, infra/vm/**), where it arrives via Secret Manager and never lands
# in a committed file.
#
# The per-chain credentials NOW EXIST, and there are TWO of them per chain — do not confuse them:
#
#   ARCHIVE  fairwins-quicknode-{ethereum,optimism,base,arbitrum}-url in Secret Manager, read by
#            the WORKSTATION as MAINNET_RPC_URL / OPTIMISM_RPC_URL / BASE_RPC_URL /
#            ARBITRUM_RPC_URL. NEVER set one of these here — it would publish it.
#   SPA      a QuickNode endpoint reaching the bundle as the four `_RPC_URL_*` substitutions on the
#            Cloud Build trigger (cloudbuild.yaml / cloudbuild.staging.yaml). They default to EMPTY,
#            so nothing is committed and an unset trigger falls through to the publicnode defaults
#            below. ⚠ THEY ARE EMPTY TODAY AND SHOULD STAY THAT WAY — see the correction below.
#
# ⚠ CORRECTION (2026-09-04). This block used to say a referrer/origin-restricted endpoint was "safe
# to publish because it is scoped rather than hidden". That is NOT what the vendor says. QuickNode's
# own endpoint-security guidance: "Domain whitelisting is not fully secure... attackers can easily
# bypass this by faking their domain using scripts" — a Referer/Origin header is set by the client,
# so the restriction stops a rival WEBSITE and does nothing against curl, a script, a server, or a
# native shell. QuickNode's stated recommendation for a frontend whose endpoint URL is publicly
# visible is to not publish the credential at all.
#
# Two further problems with publishing one, independent of the security question:
#   · ROTATION becomes a frontend release. So does adding a chain. The operation that would shorten
#     a credential's life is the one made expensive, which is how credentials end up long-lived.
#   · NOTHING VERIFIES THE RESTRICTION EXISTS. quicknode-chains.js verifies which CHAIN an endpoint
#     answers for (a wrong infix returns another chain's state rather than an error) and has no
#     notion of restrictions; check-env-hygiene.js only emits a non-failing NOTE for VITE_
#     credentials. An UNRESTRICTED archive endpoint pasted into one of these slots would ship to
#     every visitor with no gate firing.
#
# Spec 106 (specs/106-gateway-caller-auth/) replaces this path: the SPA obtains keyed read capacity
# at RUNTIME as a short-lived, read-only credential from a platform-controlled issuing point, gated
# by assurance tier — never as a build-time constant. Until that ships, leave these EMPTY and accept
# the publicnode defaults. If you believe you need to set one, read that spec's "Assumptions specific
# to keyed data access" first.
#
# See docs/developer-guide/network-endpoints.md §"QuickNode per-chain endpoints".
# VITE_RPC_URL_POLYGON=https://polygon-bor-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_RPC_URL_POLYGON_FAILOVER=https://polygon.drpc.org
# VITE_RPC_URL_ARBITRUM=https://arbitrum-one-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_RPC_URL_ARBITRUM_FAILOVER=https://arbitrum.drpc.org
# VITE_RPC_URL_BASE=https://base-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_RPC_URL_BASE_FAILOVER=https://base.drpc.org
# VITE_RPC_URL_OPTIMISM=https://optimism-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_RPC_URL_OPTIMISM_FAILOVER=https://optimism.drpc.org
# (VITE_RPC_URL_MAINNET / VITE_RPC_URL_MAINNET_FAILOVER are documented with the rest of the
#  Ethereum-family overrides directly below, since mainnet 1 belongs to both groups.)

# --- Ethereum family (mainnet 1, Sepolia 11155111, Hoodi 560048) overrides (Spec 048) ---
# All optional; networks.js ships public-RPC + canonical-token/feed defaults so the
# Ethereum family works out of the box as a value network (select, portfolio, send/receive).
# No wager/DEX/passkey infrastructure is deployed here — those capabilities stay OFF and
# self-disclose as unavailable (honest-state).
# VITE_RPC_URL_MAINNET=https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_RPC_URL_MAINNET_FAILOVER=https://eth.drpc.org
# VITE_MAINNET_USDC=0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48       # native Circle USDC, 6 decimals
# VITE_RPC_URL_SEPOLIA=https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_SEPOLIA_USDC=0x1c7D4B196Cb0C7B01d743Fbc6116a902379C7238        # Circle faucet USDC, 6 decimals
# VITE_RPC_URL_HOODI=https://ethereum-hoodi-rpc.publicnode.com
# VITE_HOODI_USDC=                                                     # leave blank until a canonical Hoodi faucet stablecoin is verified — no invented address (honest-state); Hoodi send is native-ETH-only until set
# Ethereum mainnet Chainlink price feeds (AggregatorV3, */USD, 8 decimals) — portfolio pricing.
# VITE_FEED_MAINNET_ETH_USD=0x5f4eC3Df9cbd43714FE2740f5E3616155c5b8419
# VITE_FEED_MAINNET_BTC_USD=0xF4030086522a5bEEa4988F8cA5B36dbC97BeE88c
# VITE_FEED_MAINNET_LINK_USD=0x2c1d072e956AFFC0D435Cb7AC38EF18d24d9127c

# Contract Addresses (update after deployment)
# VITE_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=0x1234567890123456789012345678901234567890

# IPFS / Pinata Configuration
# Pinata is used for uploading and pinning content to IPFS
# Get your credentials at https://app.pinata.cloud/developers/api-keys

# Pinata JWT Token — SECURITY: SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Do NOT set this as a VITE_ var in any
# deployed/production build. Vite inlines every VITE_* value into the client bundle in
# cleartext, which would leak your Pinata write credential to every visitor. Production
# IPFS uploads go through the server-side /api/pinata proxy (frontend/nginx.conf.template),
# which injects the JWT at container runtime. The production build will FAIL if this is set
# (see the pinataSecretGuard in frontend/vite.config.js). Leave it commented for builds;
# uncomment only for a local `npm run dev` that talks to Pinata directly.
# VITE_PINATA_JWT=your_pinata_jwt_token_here

# Pinata API Key and Secret (alternative to JWT)
# VITE_PINATA_API_KEY=your_pinata_api_key
# VITE_PINATA_API_SECRET=your_pinata_api_secret

# Pinata Gateway URL (optional - for dedicated gateway)
# Default uses Pinata's public gateway
# VITE_PINATA_GATEWAY=https://your-gateway.mypinata.cloud

# IPFS Gateway URL for retrieving token and market data
# Default: Uses Pinata gateway
VITE_IPFS_GATEWAY=https://gateway.pinata.cloud

# ETC/USD Price Fallback (used if CoinGecko API fails)
# VITE_ETC_USD_FALLBACK=20

# WalletConnect Configuration (Optional)
# WalletConnect is always available with a fallback project ID
# For production deployments, get your own project ID from https://cloud.walletconnect.com
# VITE_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID=your_project_id_here

# ThirdWeb Configuration (Optional)
# ThirdWeb provides enhanced wallet connection UX
# For production deployments, get your own client ID from https://thirdweb.com/dashboard
# VITE_THIRDWEB_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here

# Application URL (Required for production)
# Used for WalletConnect metadata and other external integrations
# For local development: http://localhost:5173
# For production: https://your-domain.com
VITE_APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Which oracle models the frontend exposes in the wager-creation flow + landing copy.
#   polymarket-only (default, also when unset) — show ONLY Polymarket; hide
#       Chainlink Data Feed / Chainlink Functions / UMA from the user UI.
#   all — restore every oracle model (the contracts always support them).
# Contracts/adapters are unchanged; this is a UI-visibility switch.
# VITE_ORACLE_MODELS=polymarket-only

# --- Passkey smart accounts (spec 041) ---
# Ordered, comma-separated ERC-4337 bundler endpoints per network (self-hosted
# alto first, public fallbacks). Unset => the passkey login option is hidden on
# that network (honest capability detection, FR-004).
#
# Every network below was probed with `node scripts/ops/verify-passkey-support.js`
# and can host the account stack: EntryPoint v0.6 and the Arachnid CREATE2 proxy
# are present on all of them (bytecode-hash verified). Setting the variable is
# only HALF of enabling a network — the account factory must also be deployed
# there (`scripts/deploy/deploy-account-stack.js` + `sync:frontend-contracts`),
# or the passkey option stays hidden and says so. Re-run the probe to see where
# each network stands.
#
# RIP-7212 (cheap P-256 verification) is live everywhere EXCEPT ETC and Mordor,
# which fall back to the FCL Solidity path — correct, but materially more gas per
# UserOp. Weigh that before sponsoring ops on the ETC family.
# ⚠ ONE ALTO PROCESS SERVES EXACTLY ONE CHAIN, and the origin nginx does NOT route on path
# (`location / { proxy_pass http://alto; }` — services/alto-bundler/nginx/bundler.conf.template).
# The per-chain path form these examples used to show — bundler.fairwins.app/base, /optimism, … —
# therefore all resolve to THE SAME alto. Following it points every chain at Polygon's bundler,
# where non-Polygon UserOps are simulated against Polygon state and fail with opaque validation
# errors. Nothing reports an error; the URLs look right and the bundler looks healthy.
#
# Only the chain whose alto actually answers on that host may be set. Today that is Polygon.
# Each additional chain needs its own alto instance AND its own reachable address (#1501) —
# per-chain hostname or port, decided with the rollout, never an unrouted path suffix.
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_POLYGON=https://bundler.fairwins.app
# Set only once that chain's own alto instance exists and is reachable at the URL you put here:
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_MAINNET=
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_OPTIMISM=
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_BASE=
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_ARBITRUM=
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_AMOY=
# ETC 61 / Mordor 63: out of scope — they lack opcodes that block features.
# Local dev/e2e: an alto instance pointed at the hardhat node.
# VITE_BUNDLER_URLS_LOCAL=http://127.0.0.1:4337

# FairWins-sponsored paymaster endpoint (spec 050): the relay-gateway's /v1/paymaster.
# Set => passkey UserOps are gasless (FairWins sponsors the gas; the account needs no native
# token). Unset => UserOp fees fall back to the account's native balance and the confirm UI
# discloses the native fee honestly (never a false "sponsored"). Points at the gateway, so the
# Pimlico-style API key never reaches the client. (Replaces the old VITE_ERC20_PAYMASTER_* vars.)
# Sponsorship is per-network and OPTIONAL: a network with a bundler but no paymaster still works,
# self-funded. Each one set here needs a funded FairWinsVerifyingPaymaster deposit on that chain
# and `PAYMASTER_ADDRESS_<chainId>` in the gateway — see docs/runbooks/paymaster-operations.md.
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_POLYGON=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_AMOY=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_MAINNET=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_OPTIMISM=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_BASE=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_ARBITRUM=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_ETC=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_MORDOR=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_SEPOLIA=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster
# VITE_SPONSOR_PAYMASTER_HOODI=https://relay.fairwins.app/v1/paymaster

# --- Relay-gateway clients (specs 036 / 061) ---
# The relay-gateway is one host, but WHICH client surfaces a build turns on is per-surface.
# VITE_RELAYER_URL is the shared base URL: intent relay, Predict, Perps, Collect, Solana RPC and
# the bridge quote proxy (spec 067) all read it, and Bitcoin falls back to it. Unset => gasless is
# dormant and every flow self-submits.
# VITE_RELAYER_URL=https://relay.fairwins.app
#
# Bitcoin (spec 061) reads its OWN name first and falls back to VITE_RELAYER_URL, so a build can
# point the Bitcoin client at a gateway without dragging the other clients with it (issue #1263).
# A blank value counts as unset and falls through, so an environment that sets only
# VITE_RELAYER_URL is unchanged — and, by the same rule, this variable cannot turn Bitcoin OFF:
# leaving the Bitcoin client unconfigured ("gateway unavailable", never a zero balance) means
# leaving BOTH names unset.
# VITE_BITCOIN_GATEWAY_URL=https://relay.fairwins.app

# --- Proof-of-human challenge (spec 106) ------------------------------------------------------
# Turnstile SITEKEY — public by design (the paired secret lives on the gateway as
# CHALLENGE_SECRET). Set => the app runs the widget off-screen and attaches X-FairWins-Challenge
# to gateway requests, buying the `human` tier's higher rate ceilings. Unset => fully dormant: no
# script tag, no DOM, and every caller reads at the anonymous ceiling. A challenge NEVER gates
# access — reads serve without one, always (FR-017).
# The published TEST sitekeys (1x/2x/3x…) are refused by the production build
# (challengeSitekeyGuard in vite.config.js): they always answer the same way, which makes the
# human tier a mock in a shipped path.
# VITE_CHALLENGE_SITEKEY=
